# Compound work

## Start

1. Open the plan; set Status: In progress.
2. Create the workspace:
   - Alone: `git switch -c feat/<feature-id>-<slug>` from an up-to-date `main`.
   - In parallel with other agents: `git worktree add .worktrees/<feature-id> -b feat/<feature-id>-<slug>` and work only inside that folder (it is git-ignored). Use a separate test database name; do not start a second copy of the Docker services.
3. `pnpm install` and `pnpm verify` to confirm a green starting point.

## For each task in the plan

1. Write or update the test that proves the task (from the acceptance criterion). See it fail for the right reason.
2. Implement the smallest change that makes it pass, following `AGENTS.md` and the layer rules.
3. `pnpm verify` (and the relevant E2E test if UI changed).
4. Commit: `feat(<module>): <feature-id> <what changed>` (or `fix`, `refactor`, `test`, `docs`).
5. Tick the task in the plan.

If a task turns out wrong or bigger than planned, update the plan first (with a note why), then continue. If you discover a bug outside the plan, record it as a new plan or finding instead of silently widening the scope.

## Finish

1. Update `docs/API.md`, `docs/DATABASE.md` and the PRD if behaviour changed.
2. Rebase on `main`, `pnpm verify`, run the feature's E2E and `pnpm test:security`.
3. Hand over to `pr-reviewer`; fix every P1/P2 it reports; merge.
4. Set the plan Status: Done; tick the feature in `AUTOMATION_STATUS.md`; remove the worktree (`git worktree remove .worktrees/<feature-id>`) and the merged branch.

## Compound step (do not skip)

If anything non-obvious was learned — a library quirk in this version, a pattern that worked well, a mistake that cost time — write `docs/solutions/<area>/<slug>.md`:

```
Problem / context · What we tried · What worked and why · How to avoid next time (test, lint rule, helper)
```

Short and searchable; future plans read these before starting.

Share it beyond this project too (`AUTOMATION_STATUS.md` names the automation skill folder; if that folder exists on this machine, `<skill>/scripts/learn.py` is available. If not, skip these steps.):

```bash
python <skill>/scripts/learn.py add --category <data-model|stack|security|feature|solution|process|integration|ui> \
  --title "<reusable title, no project names>" --tags <a,b,c> --project {{PROJECT_SLUG}} \
  --outcome <worked|failed> --evidence "<test file or finding id>" \
  --body "Problem: … Solution: … Use when: … Avoid when: …"
```

Record failures as well as successes, and never include secrets, customer data or internal URLs.
